      "cultural_notes_en": "French administrative email differs sharply from Vietnamese (and most English) email habits: every message must carry an « objet » (a short, specific subject line), a single opening « Madame, Monsieur, » (never « Dear Sir/Madam », never « Bonjour »), a 4–6 sentence body, and a standard formal closing « Je vous prie d'agréer… ». Get the structure wrong and your email slips to the bottom of the queue — consulate staff process hundreds of emails a day and need to recognize the format in five seconds. Don't confuse this with French corporate email (also formal but more flexible) or personal email (free-form). Consulate, préfecture, CAF, Sécurité sociale — they all use this same register. For visa requests in particular: the consulate agent is the gatekeeper of your study-abroad plan, so investing 20 minutes in a structurally perfect email matters more than the content itself.",
      "tip_advice_en": "Mandatory structure: (1) Clear objet — include visa type and academic year. (2) Salutation « Madame, Monsieur, » with the comma. (3) Body — one sentence per idea: who you are, why you're writing, current status, proposed solution. (4) Standard closing « Je vous prie d'agréer, Madame, Monsieur, l'expression de mes salutations distinguées. » (5) Signature with full name and Études en France dossier number if applicable. Avoid: emojis, exclamation marks, abbreviations, paragraphs longer than four lines. Attach only what's needed — don't spam attachments. Send from a professional email address (first.last@) — not princess99@gmail.com.",
      "roleplay_prompts_en": [
        "You need to email the French consulate requesting an urgent visa appointment — your Campus France file is complete and the September 1 start date is approaching. Write a six-line email following the standard structure.",
        "The consulate replies that all July slots are full and the earliest is September (after the academic year starts). Reply with a concrete proposal — either submitting your file by registered post, or requesting a temporary letter for your university to defer enrollment.",
        "Write a short thank-you email after your visa is granted — brief, in the correct administrative register, no flattery."
      ],
      "register_notes_en": "French administrative email to public authorities (consulate, préfecture, CAF, Sécurité sociale, university): always open with « Madame, Monsieur, » with the comma — never « Bonjour », never « Cher Monsieur ». The closing « Je vous prie d'agréer, Madame, Monsieur, l'expression de mes salutations distinguées. » is standard for a first contact or any important request. For follow-up emails in the same thread you can switch to the shorter « Cordialement ». Avoid: « salut », « hi », emojis, « lol », triple exclamation marks, ALL-CAPS sentences (reads as shouting). Don't use « tu » with public-authority staff even when speaking to a young agent on the hotline — they won't object but it signals you don't know the register."
